      "updated": 1790685166264,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "                                                DISTRIBUTED TRANSACTINS AND SAGA PATTERN\nTHE PROBLEM:\n\nIn a monolithic application with one database, a transaction is simple:\n\nsql\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'acc_1'; -- deduct from sender\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 'acc_2'; -- add to receiver\nCOMMIT; -- both succeed or both fail (atomic)\n\nIf the second UPDATE fails, the first is rolled back. The database's ACID guarantee ensures atomicity.\n\nIn microservices with separate databases, this atomicity disappears:\n\nUser places order:\n1. Order Service: Create order in Orders DB\n2. Inventory Service: Reserve stock in Inventory DB\n3. Payment Service: Charge card, record in Payments DB\n4. Notification Service: Send confirmation email\n\nIf step 3 fails:\n- Step 1 already committed (order exists)\n- Step 2 already committed (stock reserved)\n- Step 3 failed (payment didn't go through)\n- Step 4 not reached\n\nHow do you undo steps 1 and 2?\nThere's no distributed ROLLBACK.\nTwo-Phase Commit (2PC) — The Traditional Solution\n\n2PC attempts to achieve distributed atomicity using a coordinator:\n\nPHASE 1: Prepare\nCoordinator → Order Service:    \"Can you commit the order?\"\nCoordinator → Inventory Service: \"Can you reserve the stock?\"\nCoordinator → Payment Service:   \"Can you charge the card?\"\n\nEach service:\n- Locks the relevant records\n- Verifies it CAN perform the operation\n- Writes to a transaction log (for recovery)\n- Responds YES or NO\n\nPHASE 2: Commit or Abort\nIf all respond YES:\nCoordinator → Order Service:    \"COMMIT\"\nCoordinator → Inventory Service: \"COMMIT\"\nCoordinator → Payment Service:   \"COMMIT\"\nEach service releases locks and commits locally.\n\nIf any respond NO:\nCoordinator → Order Service:    \"ABORT\" (rollback)\nCoordinator → Inventory Service: \"ABORT\"\nCoordinator → Payment Service:   \"ABORT\"\nEach service releases locks without committing.\n\n                                                                WHY 2PC FAILS IN MICROSERVICES\n\n- Blocking: During phase 2, each service holds its locks. If the coordinator crashes between phases, all services are stuck holding locks indefinitely. \nResources are blocked. This is the fundamental problem with 2PC — it's a blocking protocol.\n\n- Coordinator is a SPOF: If the coordinator crashes, the whole thing is stuck.\n\n- Synchronous across services: All services must be available simultaneously. In microservices with independent deployment and failure modes, this is \nfragile.\n\n- Performance: Holding locks across a network round trip is slow. 2PC latency = multiple network round trips to all participants.\n\n- 2PC works fine within a single system (PostgreSQL uses it internally for certain operations). Across microservices, it's generally avoided.\n\n                                                        THE SAGA PATTERN — THE MODERN SOLUTION\n\nA saga is a sequence of local transactions. Each step executes a local transaction and publishes an event or message. The next step is triggered \nby that event.\n\nCritical distinction: Sagas achieve eventual consistency, not atomicity. There is a window during the saga where data is partially committed across \nservices. The saga design must handle this window gracefully.\n\nCHOREOGRAHY-BASED SAGA (EVENT-DRIVEN)\n\nEach service listens for events and decides what to do. No central coordinator.\n\nStep 1: User places order\nOrder Service → creates order (status: PENDING)\n             → publishes ORDER_CREATED event\n\nStep 2: Inventory Service receives ORDER_CREATED\nInventory Service → reserves stock\n                 → publishes STOCK_RESERVED event\n                 (or STOCK_UNAVAILABLE if out of stock)\n\nStep 3: Payment Service receives STOCK_RESERVED\nPayment Service → charges card\n              → publishes PAYMENT_PROCESSED event\n              (or PAYMENT_FAILED)\n\nStep 4: Order Service receives PAYMENT_PROCESSED\nOrder Service → updates order status to CONFIRMED\n             → publishes ORDER_CONFIRMED event\n\nStep 5: Notification Service receives ORDER_CONFIRMED\nNotification Service → sends confirmation email\n\n\nCOMPENSATING TRANSACTIONS (ROLLBACK VIA EVENTS):\nIf payment fails:\n\nPayment Service → publishes PAYMENT_FAILED event\nInventory Service → listens for PAYMENT_FAILED\n                 → releases the stock reservation\n                 → publishes STOCK_RELEASED event\nOrder Service → listens for STOCK_RELEASED (or PAYMENT_FAILED)\n             → cancels the order\n             → publishes ORDER_CANCELLED event\nNotification Service → sends \"payment failed, order cancelled\" email\n\nEach compensation event triggers the undoing of a previous step.\n\nPROS OF CHOREOGRAPHY:\n\nLoose coupling — services don't know about each other, only about events\nEasy to add new steps — new service subscribes to events without changing existing services\nResilient — no single coordinator to fail\n\nCONS:\n\nHard to understand and debug — behavior emerges from event interactions, not explicit flow\nRisk of cyclic event chains if not designed carefully\nNo global view of saga state — hard to answer \"what's the status of order 123?\"\nOrchestration-Based Saga\n\nA central saga orchestrator explicitly calls each service and handles failures.\n\nSaga Orchestrator for Order Placement:\n\nStep 1: Call Order Service\n        → If success: proceed to step 2\n        → If fail: saga fails, done\n\nStep 2: Call Inventory Service\n        → If success: proceed to step 3\n        → If fail: call Order Service to cancel order, saga fails\n\nStep 3: Call Payment Service\n        → If success: proceed to step 4\n        → If fail: call Inventory Service to release stock\n                   call Order Service to cancel order\n                   saga fails\n\nStep 4: Call Notification Service\n        → If fail: retry (notification is optional, log failure)\n\njavascript code:\n\nclass OrderSaga {\n    async execute(orderData) {\n        let orderId = null;\n        let stockReserved = false;\n\n        try {\n            // Step 1: Create order\n            const order = await orderService.createOrder(orderData);\n            orderId = order.id;\n\n            // Step 2: Reserve stock\n            await inventoryService.reserveStock(order.items);\n            stockReserved = true;\n\n            // Step 3: Process payment\n            await paymentService.chargeCard(order.total, order.paymentMethod);\n\n            // Step 4: Confirm order\n            await orderService.confirmOrder(orderId);\n\n            // Step 5: Send notification (non-critical, fire and forget)\n            notificationService.sendConfirmation(order).catch(err =>\n                logger.error('Notification failed:', err)\n            );\n\n            return { success: true, orderId };\n\n        } catch (error) {\n            // Compensating transactions\n            logger.error('Order saga failed, compensating:', error);\n\n            if (stockReserved) {\n                await inventoryService.releaseStock(orderData.items)\n                    .catch(err => logger.error('Stock release failed:', err));\n            }\n\n            if (orderId) {\n                await orderService.cancelOrder(orderId)\n                    .catch(err => logger.error('Order cancellation failed:', err));\n            }\n\n            return { success: false, error: error.message };\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n\nPROS OF ORCHESTRATION\n\n- Explicit flow — easy to understand the saga's behavior by reading the orchestrator\n- Clear error handling — the orchestrator knows the exact state and what to compensate\n- Easy to monitor — one place to track saga progress\n\nCONS:\n\n- Orchestrator knows about all services — coupling\n- Orchestrator is a central coordination point — if it fails mid-saga, the saga is stuck\n\nIn practice: Both patterns are used. Choreography for loosely coupled, independent events. Orchestration for complex, stateful workflows where \nyou need visibility and control.\n\nTHE SEMANTIC LOCK — HANDLING CONCURRENT SAGAS\n\nWhat if two sagas operate on the same data simultaneously?\n\nSaga A: Cancel order → release stock → refund payment\nSaga B: Ship order   → reduce stock  → update tracking\n\nIf these run simultaneously on the same order, you might:\n- Ship the order AND refund it\n- Reduce stock AND release stock → wrong inventory count\n\nSemantic lock: Mark the entity as \"in progress\" when a saga starts. Reject other sagas that try to operate on it.\n\njavascript code:\n\n// When saga starts:\nawait db.orders.update(orderId, { status: 'PROCESSING' });\n\n// Other sagas check before starting:\nconst order = await db.orders.findOne(orderId);\nif (order.status === 'PROCESSING') {\n    throw new Error('Order is being processed by another saga');\n}\n\nThe \"PROCESSING\" status is the semantic lock. Only one saga can hold it at a time. When the saga completes (successfully or with compensa-\ntion), the lock is released (status changes to CONFIRMED or CANCELLED).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                DISTRIBUTED TRANSACTINS AND SAGA PATTERN\nTHE PROBLEM:\n\nIn a monolithic application with one database, a transaction is simple:\n\nsql\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'acc_1'; -- deduct from sender\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 'acc_2'; -- add to receiver\nCOMMIT; -- both succeed or both fail (atomic)\n\nIf the second UPDATE fails, the first is rolled back. The database's ACID guarantee ensures atomicity.\n\nIn microservices with separate databases, this atomicity disappears:\n\nUser places order:\n1. Order Service: Create order in Orders DB\n2. Inventory Service: Reserve stock in Inventory DB\n3. Payment Service: Charge card, record in Payments DB\n4. Notification Service: Send confirmation email\n\nIf step 3 fails:\n- Step 1 already committed (order exists)\n- Step 2 already committed (stock reserved)\n- Step 3 failed (payment didn't go through)\n- Step 4 not reached\n\nHow do you undo steps 1 and 2?\nThere's no distributed ROLLBACK.\nTwo-Phase Commit (2PC) — The Traditional Solution\n\n2PC attempts to achieve distributed atomicity using a coordinator:\n\nPHASE 1: Prepare\nCoordinator → Order Service:    \"Can you commit the order?\"\nCoordinator → Inventory Service: \"Can you reserve the stock?\"\nCoordinator → Payment Service:   \"Can you charge the card?\"\n\nEach service:\n- Locks the relevant records\n- Verifies it CAN perform the operation\n- Writes to a transaction log (for recovery)\n- Responds YES or NO\n\nPHASE 2: Commit or Abort\nIf all respond YES:\nCoordinator → Order Service:    \"COMMIT\"\nCoordinator → Inventory Service: \"COMMIT\"\nCoordinator → Payment Service:   \"COMMIT\"\nEach service releases locks and commits locally.\n\nIf any respond NO:\nCoordinator → Order Service:    \"ABORT\" (rollback)\nCoordinator → Inventory Service: \"ABORT\"\nCoordinator → Payment Service:   \"ABORT\"\nEach service releases locks without committing.\n\n                                                                WHY 2PC FAILS IN MICROSERVICES\n\n- Blocking: During phase 2, each service holds its locks. If the coordinator crashes between phases, all services are stuck holding locks indefinitely. \nResources are blocked. This is the fundamental problem with 2PC — it's a blocking protocol.\n\n- Coordinator is a SPOF: If the coordinator crashes, the whole thing is stuck.\n\n- Synchronous across services: All services must be available simultaneously. In microservices with independent deployment and failure modes, this is \nfragile.\n\n- Performance: Holding locks across a network round trip is slow. 2PC latency = multiple network round trips to all participants.\n\n- 2PC works fine within a single system (PostgreSQL uses it internally for certain operations). Across microservices, it's generally avoided.\n\n                                                        THE SAGA PATTERN — THE MODERN SOLUTION\n\nA saga is a sequence of local transactions. Each step executes a local transaction and publishes an event or message. The next step is triggered \nby that event.\n\nCritical distinction: Sagas achieve eventual consistency, not atomicity. There is a window during the saga where data is partially committed across \nservices. The saga design must handle this window gracefully.\n\nCHOREOGRAHY-BASED SAGA (EVENT-DRIVEN)\n\nEach service listens for events and decides what to do. No central coordinator.\n\nStep 1: User places order\nOrder Service → creates order (status: PENDING)\n             → publishes ORDER_CREATED event\n\nStep 2: Inventory Service receives ORDER_CREATED\nInventory Service → reserves stock\n                 → publishes STOCK_RESERVED event\n                 (or STOCK_UNAVAILABLE if out of stock)\n\nStep 3: Payment Service receives STOCK_RESERVED\nPayment Service → charges card\n              → publishes PAYMENT_PROCESSED event\n              (or PAYMENT_FAILED)\n\nStep 4: Order Service receives PAYMENT_PROCESSED\nOrder Service → updates order status to CONFIRMED\n             → publishes ORDER_CONFIRMED event\n\nStep 5: Notification Service receives ORDER_CONFIRMED\nNotification Service → sends confirmation email\n\n\nCOMPENSATING TRANSACTIONS (ROLLBACK VIA EVENTS):\nIf payment fails:\n\nPayment Service → publishes PAYMENT_FAILED event\nInventory Service → listens for PAYMENT_FAILED\n                 → releases the stock reservation\n                 → publishes STOCK_RELEASED event\nOrder Service → listens for STOCK_RELEASED (or PAYMENT_FAILED)\n             → cancels the order\n             → publishes ORDER_CANCELLED event\nNotification Service → sends \"payment failed, order cancelled\" email\n\nEach compensation event triggers the undoing of a previous step.\n\nPROS OF CHOREOGRAPHY:\n\nLoose coupling — services don't know about each other, only about events\nEasy to add new steps — new service subscribes to events without changing existing services\nResilient — no single coordinator to fail\n\nCONS:\n\nHard to understand and debug — behavior emerges from event interactions, not explicit flow\nRisk of cyclic event chains if not designed carefully\nNo global view of saga state — hard to answer \"what's the status of order 123?\"\nOrchestration-Based Saga\n\nA central saga orchestrator explicitly calls each service and handles failures.\n\nSaga Orchestrator for Order Placement:\n\nStep 1: Call Order Service\n        → If success: proceed to step 2\n        → If fail: saga fails, done\n\nStep 2: Call Inventory Service\n        → If success: proceed to step 3\n        → If fail: call Order Service to cancel order, saga fails\n\nStep 3: Call Payment Service\n        → If success: proceed to step 4\n        → If fail: call Inventory Service to release stock\n                   call Order Service to cancel order\n                   saga fails\n\nStep 4: Call Notification Service\n        → If fail: retry (notification is optional, log failure)\n\njavascript code:\n\nclass OrderSaga {\n    async execute(orderData) {\n        let orderId = null;\n        let stockReserved = false;\n\n        try {\n            // Step 1: Create order\n            const order = await orderService.createOrder(orderData);\n            orderId = order.id;\n\n            // Step 2: Reserve stock\n            await inventoryService.reserveStock(order.items);\n            stockReserved = true;\n\n            // Step 3: Process payment\n            await paymentService.chargeCard(order.total, order.paymentMethod);\n\n            // Step 4: Confirm order\n            await orderService.confirmOrder(orderId);\n\n            // Step 5: Send notification (non-critical, fire and forget)\n            notificationService.sendConfirmation(order).catch(err =>\n                logger.error('Notification failed:', err)\n            );\n\n            return { success: true, orderId };\n\n        } catch (error) {\n            // Compensating transactions\n            logger.error('Order saga failed, compensating:', error);\n\n            if (stockReserved) {\n                await inventoryService.releaseStock(orderData.items)\n                    .catch(err => logger.error('Stock release failed:', err));\n            }\n\n            if (orderId) {\n                await orderService.cancelOrder(orderId)\n                    .catch(err => logger.error('Order cancellation failed:', err));\n            }\n\n            return { success: false, error: error.message };\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n\nPROS OF ORCHESTRATION\n\n- Explicit flow — easy to understand the saga's behavior by reading the orchestrator\n- Clear error handling — the orchestrator knows the exact state and what to compensate\n- Easy to monitor — one place to track saga progress\n\nCONS:\n\n- Orchestrator knows about all services — coupling\n- Orchestrator is a central coordination point — if it fails mid-saga, the saga is stuck\n\nIn practice: Both patterns are used. Choreography for loosely coupled, independent events. Orchestration for complex, stateful workflows where \nyou need visibility and control.\n\nTHE SEMANTIC LOCK — HANDLING CONCURRENT SAGAS\n\nWhat if two sagas operate on the same data simultaneously?\n\nSaga A: Cancel order → release stock → refund payment\nSaga B: Ship order   → reduce stock  → update tracking\n\nIf these run simultaneously on the same order, you might:\n- Ship the order AND refund it\n- Reduce stock AND release stock → wrong inventory count\n\nSemantic lock: Mark the entity as \"in progress\" when a saga starts. Reject other sagas that try to operate on it.\n\njavascript code:\n\n// When saga starts:\nawait db.orders.update(orderId, { status: 'PROCESSING' });\n\n// Other sagas check before starting:\nconst order = await db.orders.findOne(orderId);\nif (order.status === 'PROCESSING') {\n    throw new Error('Order is being processed by another saga');\n}\n\nThe \"PROCESSING\" status is the semantic lock. Only one saga can hold it at a time. When the saga completes (successfully or with compensa-\ntion), the lock is released (status changes to CONFIRMED or CANCELLED).",
